#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate 200 premium Behavioral Economics Q&A pairs — Upgrade K"""
import json, pathlib

OUT = pathlib.Path('/workspace/data_upgrades')
OUT.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

pairs = [
    ("What is behavioral economics?", "Behavioral economics combines insights from psychology and economics to explain why people often make decisions that deviate from the predictions of classical rational choice theory. It documents systematic cognitive biases, emotional influences, and social pressures that shape economic decisions, and uses these insights to design better policies and choice environments (nudges)."),
    ("What is the homo economicus model?", "Homo economicus ('economic man') is the classical model assuming agents have stable, well-defined preferences; process all available information rationally; maximise utility or profit; and act in narrow self-interest. Behavioral economics challenges this as a descriptive model, showing that real people use heuristics, have limited attention and willpower, and are influenced by social norms and framing."),
    ("What is bounded rationality?", "Herbert Simon's bounded rationality: real decision-making is constrained by limited information, cognitive capacity, and time. People satisfice — choose the first option that meets a satisfactory threshold — rather than optimise. Heuristics (mental shortcuts) produce adequate results in most cases but generate systematic errors. The mind adapts to cognitive limits by using fast-and-frugal heuristics."),
    ("What is loss aversion in behavioral economics?", "Kahneman and Tversky's prospect theory found that losses are felt approximately twice as painfully as equivalent gains are pleasurable. Loss aversion drives: status quo bias (avoiding changes that might entail losses); endowment effect (overvaluing owned objects); sunk cost fallacy (continuing a losing project to avoid realising a loss); and risk-seeking to avoid certain losses."),
    ("What is the endowment effect?", "The endowment effect (Thaler, Kahneman, Knetsch): people demand significantly more to give up an object they own than they would pay to acquire it. In classic mug experiments, owners demanded ~$7 but buyers offered ~$3. Loss aversion explains it: losing the owned mug feels worse than the opportunity cost of not having a new one. It underlies status quo bias and negotiation anchoring."),
    ("What is the status quo bias?", "Status quo bias is the preference for the current state of affairs — changes from the reference point are perceived as losses. Samuelson and Zeckhauser documented it in retirement plan and insurance choices. It contributes to inertia in pension contributions, political conservatism, and consumer brand loyalty. Default options exploit status quo bias — by making the desired behaviour the default, uptake increases dramatically."),
    ("What is the sunk cost fallacy?", "The sunk cost fallacy is continuing a course of action because of past investments of money, time, or effort that cannot be recovered — even when continuing is suboptimal going forward. Rational analysis should only consider future costs and benefits. Sunk costs are irrelevant. Behaviorally, sunk costs create an emotional reluctance to 'waste' past investment. Examples: continuing a failing war, completing a bad movie, and escalation of commitment in business."),
    ("What is the availability heuristic?", "People estimate the probability of events by how easily examples come to mind. Vivid, recent, or emotionally salient events are more available. This causes: overestimation of dramatic risks (plane crashes, terrorism) relative to mundane ones (car accidents, heart disease); recency bias in financial markets; and media influence on risk perception. Availability is adaptive but distorted by salience and memorability."),
    ("What is the representativeness heuristic?", "Representativeness: judging how likely something is by how closely it resembles a prototype or stereotype, ignoring base rates. The Linda problem (Kahneman): 'Linda is a bank teller who is also a feminist activist' rated more probable than 'Linda is a bank teller' — the conjunction fallacy. Stereotype thinking, hot-hand fallacy (basketball streaks), and gamblers' fallacy all reflect representativeness reasoning."),
    ("What is the anchoring effect?", "Anchoring is the tendency to rely heavily on the first piece of information offered (the anchor) when making decisions. Even arbitrary anchors influence numerical estimates. Shown in: salary negotiations (first offer anchors outcome); legal sentencing (prosecutors' requests anchor judge's sentences); house price listing (asking price anchors purchase offers); and fundraising (suggested donation amounts anchor gifts)."),
    ("What is framing effect?", "The framing effect: people's choices change depending on whether options are presented in terms of gains or losses, even when the information is objectively identical. Asian Disease Problem: '400 saved' (positive frame) is preferred over 'equivalent option,' but '200 die' (negative frame) of the same option is avoided. Framing affects medical treatment choices, investment decisions, and policy support."),
    ("What is mental accounting?", "Mental accounting (Thaler): people categorise money into separate psychological accounts and treat money differently depending on its source, intended use, or account. This leads to: not treating £100 found differently from £100 earned; spending a tax refund more readily than regular income (windfall effect); and reluctance to sell losing stocks (realising a loss in the investment account)."),
    ("What is the house money effect?", "The house money effect (Thaler and Johnson): people are more willing to take risks with recently won money ('house money') than with their own capital, because winnings are mentally categorised separately and feel like free money. It explains risk-seeking behaviour after gains in gambling, investment, and sport betting. The opposite — loss aversion — dominates after losses."),
    ("What is the disposition effect?", "The disposition effect (Shefrin and Statman) is investors' tendency to sell winning investments too quickly (realising gains) and hold losing investments too long (avoiding realising losses). It conflicts with rational portfolio management. Driven by loss aversion and mental accounting — investors do not want to close a loss account. The disposition effect reduces investment returns and is linked to overconfidence."),
    ("What is overconfidence in behavioral economics?", "Overconfidence manifests as: calibration overconfidence (confidence intervals are too narrow — people say 90% confident but are right only 70% of the time); better-than-average effect (most people rate themselves above average in driving, intelligence); and illusion of control (believing one has control over random outcomes). It drives excessive trading, entrepreneurial entry, and risk-taking."),
    ("What is the planning fallacy?", "People underestimate the time, cost, and risk of tasks while overestimating benefits — even when they know similar past projects ran over. The inside view (focusing on the specific project) dominates the outside view (base rate for similar projects). Kahneman and Tversky identified it; Bent Flyvbjerg documented it in infrastructure projects. Reference class forecasting (outside view) corrects the bias."),
    ("What is optimism bias?", "The optimism bias (Sharot): people overestimate the probability of positive future events and underestimate the probability of negative ones — believing they are less likely than average to experience divorce, illness, or accidents. It underlies entrepreneurial overconfidence, financial over-leveraging, and failure to purchase insurance. The brain selectively updates beliefs from positive rather than negative information."),
    ("What is hyperbolic discounting?", "People are time-inconsistent: they heavily discount the immediate future relative to the more distant future but have more moderate preferences among future periods. 'I prefer £100 today to £110 tomorrow but prefer £110 in 31 days to £100 in 30 days.' This produces present bias — valuing immediate rewards disproportionately. It explains procrastination, under-saving, overeating, and addiction."),
    ("What is present bias?", "Present bias is the tendency to overweight immediate payoffs relative to future ones, in a way that is time-inconsistent. Naïve present-biased agents do not anticipate their future preference reversals; sophisticated agents do, and may seek commitment devices. It drives failure to exercise, undersaving for retirement, procrastination, and substance abuse. O'Donoghue and Rabin formalised it with quasi-hyperbolic discounting (beta-delta model)."),
    ("What is a commitment device?", "A commitment device is a mechanism that helps individuals follow through on their intentions by restricting future choices or imposing costs on self-defeating behaviour. Examples: retirement savings locked until retirement (401k early withdrawal penalty); gym contracts; Ulysses tying himself to the mast; website blockers; staggered bonuses for weight loss (Ariely). Commitment devices are valuable for sophisticated, present-biased agents."),
    ("What is a nudge?", "A nudge (Thaler and Sunstein) is any aspect of the choice architecture that alters behaviour in a predictable way without forbidding options or significantly changing economic incentives. Examples: defaulting employees into pension saving (opt-out rather than opt-in); placing healthy food first in cafeteria lines; showing energy use relative to neighbours. Nudges preserve freedom of choice (libertarian paternalism) while improving outcomes."),
    ("What is libertarian paternalism?", "Libertarian paternalism (Thaler and Sunstein): the idea that it is possible to steer people toward better choices (paternalism) while preserving their freedom to choose otherwise (libertarian). The government or employer designs choice architectures with 'good' defaults without prohibiting alternatives. Nudge theory is its implementation. Critics argue it is manipulative or paternalistic; proponents note that choice architecture is unavoidable."),
    ("What is choice architecture?", "Choice architecture is the design of the environment in which people make decisions. Every detail influences choices: the order of options (primacy effect), defaults, the framing of information, the number of choices (choice overload), and physical salience. Retailers, policymakers, and employers routinely use choice architecture — the insight of behavioral economics is to do so deliberately and transparently to improve outcomes."),
    ("What is the default effect?", "Default options exert enormous influence because of status quo bias — people tend not to deviate from the default. Organ donation rates are dramatically higher in opt-out countries (85-99%) than opt-in countries (10-30%) even with identical information. Default savings rates in pension plans heavily influence retirement saving. Defaults represent an implicit policy choice that policymakers should make deliberately."),
    ("What is the SAVE MORE TOMORROW plan?", "Thaler and Benartzi's SMarT (Save More Tomorrow) plan: employees commit in advance to allocating a portion of future pay raises to retirement savings, with automatic escalation each year. By committing in advance (reducing present-bias effect) and framing savings as a portion of raises (not pay cuts), SMarT dramatically increased savings rates in field experiments. It is now widely implemented in US 401(k) plans."),
    ("What is choice overload?", "The paradox of choice (Iyengar and Lepper): when offered more options, people are less likely to make any choice, and when they do choose, they are less satisfied. The jam study: a display of 24 jams attracted more customers but generated 1/10 the sales of a 6-jam display. Too many options create cognitive overload and decision paralysis. Optimal design limits meaningful alternatives while preserving real diversity."),
    ("What is attribute substitution?", "When a judgment is difficult, people substitute an easier related question. 'Would you buy this stock?' (hard) is answered by 'How do I feel about this company?' (easy emotional question). The answer to the surrogate question is used as the answer to the target question. This underlies many heuristics: availability (substituting ease of recall for frequency) and representativeness (substituting similarity for probability)."),
    ("What is the affect heuristic?", "The affect heuristic: emotional associations guide judgements about risk and benefit. People perceive high-risk activities as having low benefit and low-risk activities as having high benefit — even when the two are uncorrelated in reality. If you like nuclear power, you rate it as low risk and high benefit; if you dislike it, high risk and low benefit. Emotional priming shifts risk perception independently of information."),
    ("What is the peak-end rule in behavioral economics?", "Kahneman's peak-end rule: people evaluate experiences based on the most intense moment (peak — which may be positive or negative) and the final moment (end), not the integral of experience over time (duration neglect). Shorter but worse experiences with better endings can be preferred over longer better experiences with worse endings. Implications for product design, medical procedures, and customer service."),
    ("What is duration neglect?", "In evaluating experiences, people largely ignore how long an experience lasted — they focus on the peak and the end. In cold water experiments (Kahneman), subjects preferred a 90-second immersion (60 seconds of cold water + 30 seconds of slightly warmer water) over a shorter 60-second immersion, even though the longer one involved all the same pain plus more. Duration adds to objective discomfort but not remembered discomfort."),
    ("What is the IKEA effect?", "The IKEA effect (Norton, Mochon, Ariely): people place disproportionately high value on things they help create — self-assembled IKEA furniture, homemade food, and self-built models are valued more highly than objectively identical items made by others. It reflects the connection between labour and pride of ownership. Marketers exploit it through mass customisation and co-creation."),
    ("What is the identifiable victim effect?", "People are more generous toward an identified individual ('Rokia, a 7-year-old girl from Mali') than toward statistical victims ('millions of children face famine'). Singularity effect: as the number of identified victims increases, charitable donations per victim fall. This distorts aid allocation toward salient, identifiable victims and away from the most cost-effective causes."),
    ("What is scope insensitivity?", "People's willingness to pay is insensitive to the scale of an environmental problem or number of lives saved. Willingness to pay to save 2,000, 20,000, or 200,000 birds from oil spills is roughly constant — not proportional to the magnitude. This is the scope neglect or scope insensitivity effect. It undermines cost-benefit analysis of large-scale problems and climate policy."),
    ("What is social norms in behavioral economics?", "Descriptive norms (what most people do) and injunctive norms (what most people approve of) powerfully influence behaviour. OPOWER's energy reports showing households their consumption relative to similar neighbours reduced energy use by 2% — as effective as a 20% price increase. Social norm feedback harnesses conformity and the desire for positive social image."),
    ("What is the social preference for fairness?", "People care about fair outcomes, not just self-interest. Ultimatum game: one player proposes how to split a sum; the other accepts or rejects. Rational self-interest predicts acceptance of any positive offer. In reality, offers below 30% are typically rejected — punishing unfairness at personal cost. People have fairness preferences: inequity aversion (Fehr and Schmidt), strong reciprocity, and willingness to punish norm violators."),
    ("What is the ultimatum game?", "In the ultimatum game, Player 1 proposes a split of, say, $10; Player 2 accepts (both get money) or rejects (both get nothing). Self-interest predicts Player 2 accepts any positive offer. In practice, offers below $3 are frequently rejected across cultures — punishment of unfairness, even at cost. Player 1 anticipates this and typically offers 40-50%. The game reveals social preferences for fairness and reciprocity."),
    ("What is the dictator game?", "In the dictator game, Player 1 unilaterally decides how to split a sum; Player 2 receives whatever is allocated (cannot reject). Self-interest predicts Player 1 keeps everything. In reality, most dictators give some amount (average ~20-28%), demonstrating altruism and fairness preferences. The game shows these preferences exist even absent strategic considerations — not merely fear of rejection as in the ultimatum game."),
    ("What is the trust game?", "Player 1 sends money to Player 2; the amount is tripled by the experimenter; Player 2 decides how much to return. Rational self-interest: Player 2 never returns money; Player 1 sends nothing. In practice, Player 1 typically sends half and Player 2 returns roughly what was sent — demonstrating trust and trustworthiness. Oxytocin administration (Zak) increases trust. The game models investment, employment, and social cooperation."),
    ("What is altruistic punishment?", "Altruistic punishment is the willingness to bear personal costs to punish norm violators — even when there is no direct benefit and the punisher will never interact with the subject again. Found in public goods games and third-party punishment experiments. It sustains cooperation by deterring free-riding. Neuroeconomics finds that punishment activates reward circuitry in the brain, suggesting punishment can be intrinsically satisfying."),
    ("What is the public goods game?", "In the public goods game, players contribute to a common pool that is multiplied and divided equally among all. The socially optimal outcome is full contribution; the individual incentive is to contribute nothing (free ride). Typically, contributions start high and decline as free-riders are tolerated. With punishment options, cooperation is sustained. The game models common resource management, climate agreements, and tax compliance."),
    ("What is reference dependence?", "Prospect theory's reference dependence: people evaluate outcomes relative to a reference point (typically the status quo), not in absolute terms. Gains and losses are defined relative to this point. Reference points can be shifted — by what sellers emphasise, by recent experience, or by social comparison. Reference dependence explains the endowment effect, loss aversion, and anchoring."),
    ("What is the certainty effect?", "The certainty effect (Kahneman and Tversky): people disproportionately weight certain outcomes relative to merely probable outcomes of similar expected value. Allais paradox: most people prefer a certainty of 3M over a 0.8 chance of 4M (violating expected utility) but prefer 0.2 chance of 4M over 0.25 chance of 3M (consistent with EUT). Certainty is overweighted; probability weighting is not linear."),
    ("What is the fourfold pattern in prospect theory?", "Prospect theory predicts four cells: (1) high probability gains — risk-averse (certainty effect — prefer sure gain); (2) low probability gains — risk-seeking (overweight small probabilities — lottery tickets); (3) high probability losses — risk-seeking (prefer gamble over certain loss); (4) low probability losses — risk-averse (overweight small probabilities — insurance). This fourfold pattern is well-documented."),
    ("What is probability weighting?", "Prospect theory replaces objective probabilities with subjective decision weights. Small probabilities are overweighted (explaining lottery play and insurance purchase); large probabilities are underweighted (explaining the certainty effect). The weighting function is inverse-S shaped — steep at the extremes. This probability distortion is partly responsible for violations of expected utility theory."),
    ("What is regret theory?", "Regret theory (Loomes and Sugden, Bell): decision utility includes anticipated regret from comparing outcomes with what would have occurred under alternative choices. People avoid actions that might lead to anticipated regret, even at significant expected-value cost. Counterfactual thinking amplifies regret for near-misses. Regret motivates market herding (blaming others rather than feeling regret) and portfolio inertia."),
    ("What is ambiguity aversion?", "Ellsberg paradox: people prefer known probabilities (risk) to unknown probabilities (ambiguity). Given urns with known vs unknown red/blue ball ratios, people prefer the urn with known composition regardless of the direction of the bet — violating subjective expected utility theory. Ambiguity aversion increases in unfamiliar domains and explains home bias in investment and conservatism in medical decision-making."),
    ("What is the Ellsberg paradox?", "Ellsberg's thought experiment: Urn 1 has 50 red and 50 black balls. Urn 2 has 100 balls of unknown mixture. Most people prefer Urn 1 for a bet on red AND for a bet on black — which violates Bayesian updating (if you'd assign 50% to red in Urn 2, you should be indifferent). Ambiguity aversion explains this: people prefer the urn where probabilities are known (risk) over unknown ones (uncertainty)."),
    ("What is the hot-hand fallacy?", "The hot-hand fallacy is the belief that a person who has recent successes is more likely to succeed in the next attempt — for example, believing a basketball player on a 'streak' is more likely to make the next shot. Gilovich, Vallone, and Tversky showed that basketball shooting sequences are consistent with independent random events. The pattern-seeking brain sees streaks in random data. A 2016 reanalysis found small genuine hot-hand effects in some contexts."),
    ("What is the gambler's fallacy?", "The gambler's fallacy is the incorrect belief that after a run of independent random events in one direction, the opposite outcome is 'due.' Example: after five coin flip 'heads,' believing 'tails' is more likely. The opposite of the hot-hand fallacy. Both reflect the belief that random sequences should 'look' representative — local representativeness. In truth, independent events have no memory."),
    ("What is the base rate fallacy?", "Ignoring statistical base rates in favour of specific information (availability or representativeness). Example: a person described as 'shy and bookish' is guessed to be a librarian even when the base rate for engineers vastly exceeds librarians in the population. Medical diagnosis: Bayes' theorem requires combining test sensitivity with disease prevalence (base rate) — false-positive rates are greatly underestimated without this."),
    ("What is the law of small numbers?", "People overgeneralise from small samples, treating them as if they were large. Small samples exhibit high variability, but people expect them to be highly representative of the population (belief in the law of small numbers). This causes: seeing meaningful patterns in small sample data; inferring talent from short sequences of performance; and misinterpreting cancer cluster maps."),
    ("What is regression to the mean?", "Extreme scores on any measure tend to be followed by scores closer to the mean on subsequent measurement — regression to the mean (Galton). This occurs because extreme performance combines ability with luck; subsequent performance reflects ability alone. Misattributing regression effects causes: thinking praise harms subsequent performance; medical treatments appearing to work for extreme patients; and 'Sports Illustrated jinx.'"),
    ("What is inattentional blindness in economics?", "Attention is scarce. People focus on salient cues and miss important background information. In economic decisions: hidden fees are not considered; small print is ignored; complex financial products exploit inattention. Thaler's 'attention tax' — when information must be sought, many ignore it. Behaviorally informed disclosure (bigger fonts, simpler language, key facts boxes) is more effective than requiring disclosure alone."),
    ("What is the flat-rate bias?", "Consumers prefer flat-rate pricing (unlimited plans) over usage-based pricing, even when they consistently use less than the flat-rate amount — they overpay. This reflects risk aversion and desire for predictability (insurance motive), overestimation of future usage, and the 'taxi meter effect' (metered costs reduce enjoyment). Mobile phone, gym, and internet providers exploit flat-rate bias."),
    ("What is gym membership paradox?", "DellaVigna and Malmendier found gym members paying monthly fees attended far less than their cost-per-visit implied they expected to, and members on per-visit plans attended more cost-effectively. Monthly members systematically over-predicted their own attendance — overconfidence combined with present bias. The gym membership paradox illustrates how firms profit from consumers' self-control problems and optimistic beliefs."),
    ("What is the winner's curse?", "In common-value auctions (oil field leases, M&A), the winner is the bidder who bid highest — who most overestimated the value. Rational bidders should shade their bids below their estimate to account for this. In practice, bidders fail to adjust sufficiently, leading to overpayment. The winner's curse explains why M&A acquirers often pay too much and oil field auction winners frequently lose money."),
    ("What is herd behavior in financial markets?", "Herd behavior: investors follow others' actions rather than their own information — causing asset bubbles and crashes. Information cascades: when individuals observe others' actions but not their private signals, they rationally ignore their own information and imitate. Reputational herding: fund managers mimic each other to avoid being wrong alone. Social proof makes herding feel safe; contrarianism requires resisting social influence."),
    ("What is animal spirits in economics?", "Keynes' 'animal spirits' captures non-rational forces — confidence, fear, and narrative — that drive investment and economic fluctuations beyond what fundamental analysis warrants. Akerlof and Shiller revived the term: animal spirits include confidence, fairness, corruption, money illusion, and the importance of stories in economic behaviour. They argue macroeconomics must account for these psychological drivers of aggregate demand."),
    ("What is money illusion?", "Money illusion is the confusion of nominal and real values — treating a nominal pay cut as worse than an equivalent real pay cut due to inflation. People prefer a 2% nominal raise when inflation is 4% (real cut of 2%) over a 0% nominal raise when inflation is 0% (real raise of 0%) — even though the latter is better in real terms. Employers exploit money illusion to cut real wages through inflation during downturns."),
    ("What is the price-quality heuristic?", "People use price as a signal of quality — assuming more expensive items are higher quality, especially for unfamiliar products or complex goods. Blind wine tastings show more expensive wines are rated higher even by experts (Plassmann neuroimaging). Cheap aspirin is less effective at reducing pain than expensive branded aspirin in some studies. The brain's reward response to products is partly driven by expected quality from price cues."),
    ("What is the anchoring in wage negotiations?", "The first salary offer in a negotiation serves as an anchor. Research shows: initial offers strongly predict final settlements; making the first offer (if well-informed) is advantageous; and irrelevant anchors (asking 'Is your salary more or less than $X?') influence subsequent self-reported salary expectations. Aggressive initial anchors raise final outcomes; failing to move far enough from anchors is a common negotiating error."),
    ("What is the decoy effect?", "The decoy effect (compromise effect): adding an asymmetrically dominated option (decoy) increases preference for the superior option. Classic case: a $2 magazine subscription, a $125 website subscription, and a $125 magazine + web subscription. The third option (same price as second, more content) makes the second look like poor value and dramatically increases take-up of the third. The decoy is not chosen but shifts preferences toward the target."),
    ("What is the compromise effect?", "When choosing between options, people tend to select the middle option — avoiding extremes. Marketers use this: introducing a premium product makes the previous 'expensive' product look like a moderate choice, increasing its sales. Wine lists price a 'house wine' cheaply to make the second-cheapest wine (the target) look like moderate, sensible value. The compromise effect is a context effect — preferences are not stable."),
    ("What is the spotlight effect?", "The spotlight effect: people overestimate how much others notice their appearance or behaviour. They feel as if they are under a spotlight. Gilovich found that students wearing an embarrassing T-shirt greatly overestimated how many classmates noticed it. The spotlight effect causes social anxiety, reluctance to try new things, and overconfidence in one's social impressions. Awareness reduces distress."),
    ("What is the illusion of transparency?", "People overestimate how much their internal states are visible to others — believing their nervousness, boredom, or embarrassment are apparent when they are not. Like the spotlight effect, it reflects excessive self-focus. Speakers overestimate how much their nervousness shows; poker players overestimate how easily others read their 'tells.' The illusion stems from the asymmetry between our vivid internal experience and others' limited perception."),
    ("What is choice bracketing?", "Narrow vs broad bracketing affects decisions. Investors who evaluate their portfolio daily experience more losses (negative affect) than those who evaluate annually — narrow bracketing amplifies loss aversion. Samuelson's coin-flip paradox: refusing a bet with positive expected value but accepting 100 sequential bets — broad bracketing reveals the long-run advantage. Pension plans encourage broad bracketing (don't check the balance daily)."),
    ("What is the behavioral life-cycle hypothesis?", "Modigliani's life-cycle hypothesis (rational): people smooth consumption across their lifetime. Thaler's behavioral life-cycle hypothesis: people treat income as belonging to separate mental accounts — current income, current wealth, and future income — and have higher marginal propensity to consume out of current income and windfall gains than out of future wealth. This explains why the poor have high consumption rates and the rich save more than models predict."),
    ("What is debt aversion?", "People are reluctant to take on debt even when it is financially beneficial — they dislike the psychological burden of owing money more than the objective cost warrants. This causes: under-investment in high-return education (student loan aversion); failure to use credit even when rates are low; and over-repayment of low-interest mortgage debt before investing. Debt aversion interacts with mental accounting of the 'debt account.'"),
    ("What is the psychology of poverty?", "Mullainathan and Shafir's scarcity theory: scarcity (of money, time, or food) captures attention and creates a cognitive tunnel focus — solving the immediate problem but causing neglect of other important matters ('tunneling'). Scarcity reduces cognitive bandwidth available for self-control and long-term planning. Poverty perpetuates itself partly through cognitive load, not just material deprivation. Policy: reduce cognitive burden (automatic enrollment, simplified forms)."),
    ("What is present bias and retirement saving?", "Present bias causes systematic undersaving for retirement — future well-being is steeply discounted. Rational models predict people will save optimally; behavioral evidence shows most people save far less than they intend. Solutions: automatic enrollment in pension plans (Madrian and Shea — default enrollment tripled participation); automatic escalation (SMarT plan); targeted reminders; and commitment savings accounts."),
    ("What is behavioral contract theory?", "Classical contract theory assumes rational parties design optimal contracts. Behavioral contract theory incorporates: limited cognition (parties cannot write complete contingent contracts); overconfidence (over-optimism about performance); loss aversion (asymmetric risk-sharing); and present bias (short-term incentives dominate long-term ones). Optimal contract design must account for employees' and employers' behavioral biases, not just information asymmetry."),
    ("What is the overjustification effect in labor markets?", "Adding extrinsic rewards (bonuses) to intrinsically motivating work can crowd out intrinsic motivation, reducing effort when the reward is withdrawn. Gneezy and Rustichini (2000): daycare centres that fined parents for late pickup saw an increase in late pickups — the fine converted a social obligation into a market transaction. Crowding-out is most severe for pro-social and creative tasks; in routine tasks, monetary incentives generally increase effort."),
    ("What is reference-dependent labor supply?", "Taxi drivers and bike messengers set daily income targets and quit work once the target is reached — even on days with high demand and high hourly rates, they stop working earlier than on low-demand days. This violates standard labour supply theory (work more when wages are high). It reflects reference-dependent utility: reaching the income reference point feels like a gain (quit); falling short feels like a loss (work hard). Camerer et al. (1997)."),
    ("What is the hot stove effect in economics?", "After a negative experience with a brand, firm, or investment, people avoid it far more than the rational update of their beliefs warrants. The reverse — positive surprise — generates less loyalty gain. The asymmetry reflects loss aversion: bad experiences are weighted more heavily than equally surprising good ones. Marketing must work disproportionately hard to recover from negative events."),
    ("What is salience theory in economics?", "Bordalo, Gennaioli, and Shleifer's salience theory: when choosing between options, attention focuses on attributes that are most different across options (salient attributes). If one product is cheapest, price becomes salient; if products have different features, quality becomes salient. Attention to the salient attribute distorts choice away from expected utility maximisation. It explains context effects and framing in consumer choice."),
    ("What is the behavioral finance view of market anomalies?", "Behavioral finance explains market anomalies that challenge the efficient market hypothesis: momentum (recent winners continue winning — investor underreaction); reversal (long-term losers outperform — investor overreaction); the value premium (cheap stocks outperform expensive ones); post-earnings announcement drift; IPO underperformance; and size premium. These arise from biases: overconfidence, representativeness, loss aversion, and herding."),
    ("What is the disposition effect in behavioral finance?", "The disposition effect explains why investors sell winners too soon and hold losers too long — driven by loss aversion and prospect theory's S-shaped value function. In the loss domain (below purchase price), the value function is concave and investors are risk-seeking (hold the loser hoping to recover). In the gain domain (above purchase price), the function is convex and investors are risk-averse (sell the winner to lock in the gain)."),
    ("What is overtrading in financial markets?", "Barber and Odean found that individual investors who traded most frequently earned the lowest net returns — trading costs and poor timing eroded profits. Overconfidence drives overtrading: investors overestimate the quality of their information and the precision of their forecasts. Men traded more than women and underperformed more — consistent with overconfidence findings. Online trading exacerbated overtrading by reducing barriers."),
    ("What is belief perseverance?", "Belief perseverance is the tendency to hold onto initial beliefs even when presented with contradictory evidence. Once a hypothesis is formed and supported, confirming evidence is more influential than disconfirming evidence. In financial markets, analysts revise forecasts sluggishly in response to contradictory information — contributing to post-earnings announcement drift. Motivated reasoning amplifies perseverance for self-serving beliefs."),
    ("What is the behavioral approach to consumer credit?", "Standard models: rational consumers maximise utility by timing credit use optimally. Behavioral reality: present bias leads to excessive credit card borrowing (immediate spending, future pain of repayment); complexity of credit card terms reduces comparison shopping; minimum payment anchoring (minimum payment listed on statement anchors payments lower — UK field experiment by Stewart et al.); and mental accounting separates credit card debt from savings accounts."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of health?", "People systematically fail to make health-optimal decisions: present bias drives overeating (immediate pleasure vs future health cost); optimism bias causes under-insurance and failure to screen; status quo bias prevents uptake of healthy behaviours; and habit formation keeps people on unhealthy paths. Behavioral interventions: calorie labelling (reduces consumption marginally); commitment contracts (GymPact); defaults (healthier cafeteria options first)."),
    ("What is the affect bridge in consumer behavior?", "Affective responses to prior events transfer to subsequent stimuli — the affect bridge. Background mood states influence product evaluations: people in good moods rate products higher; classical music in wine stores increases purchases of expensive wine (North). Affect from sports team outcomes influences stock market returns on the following day (Edmans). Emotional contagion from media and social interactions shapes economic decisions."),
    ("What is conditional cooperation?", "In public goods and cooperation dilemmas, people are conditional cooperators — they cooperate as long as others do, but reduce cooperation when they observe free-riding. This creates two stable equilibria: full cooperation (when everyone cooperates) and full defection (when free-riding is observed). Feedback about others' contributions, social norm framing, and punishment mechanisms sustain cooperation by preventing the unravelling from conditional cooperators."),
    ("What is bounded self-control?", "Thaler and Shefrin proposed the planner-doer model: the individual has a far-sighted 'planner' who wants to maximise long-run utility and a myopic 'doer' who pursues immediate satisfaction. Self-control problems arise when the doer dominates. Rules (spending limits), commitment devices (automatic saving), temptation bundling (gym + podcasts), and environmental design (removing unhealthy foods) help the planner control the doer."),
    ("What is temptation bundling?", "Temptation bundling (Milkman): pairing immediately pleasurable but 'guilty' activities with beneficial but effortful ones. Giving audiobooks only at the gym increased gym visits; allowing oneself to watch favourite TV shows only while exercising sustainably increased activity. Bundling harnesses present bias (immediate reward) to overcome procrastination and self-control failure on beneficial-but-delayed-reward activities."),
    ("What is the effect of deadlines on procrastination?", "Ariely and Wertenbroch: evenly spaced self-imposed deadlines improved academic performance relative to end-of-term deadlines — even though self-imposed deadlines were non-binding. People with no deadlines clustered all work at the end (procrastination). Deadlines serve as commitment devices against present-bias-driven delay. Optimal deadline design spaces checkpoints throughout a project rather than placing all pressure at the end."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of charitable giving?", "Warm-glow (Andreoni): people donate partly for the good feeling of giving, not just the public good. This explains why donations persist despite free-riding predictions. Identifiable victim effect: people give more to a specific named victim than to statistics. Matching grants increase donations by signalling impact and social proof. Default giving (opt-out charitable contributions) dramatically increases donations."),
    ("What is the tax compliance behavioral economics?", "Standard deterrence model: people comply when expected fine exceeds tax due. Behavioral evidence: compliance is far higher than deterrence alone predicts — social norms matter. HMRC 'many of your neighbours have paid' letter increased payment rates by 5% (Behavioural Insights Team). Tax salience: income taxes are less salient when withheld from wages (reducing resistance) than when paid as a lump sum."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of insurance?", "Insurance purchase violates expected utility theory in multiple ways: low-probability, high-severity events are overweighted (flood insurance), but high-probability, low-severity events are underweighted (dental coverage). Extended warranties on appliances are overpriced but widely purchased — availability of breakdown memories, loss aversion. Behaviorally, insurance is partly about eliminating worry, not objective risk pooling."),
    ("What is the nudge unit (Behavioural Insights Team)?", "The UK Behavioural Insights Team (BIT, 'Nudge Unit'), founded 2010, applies behavioral economics to public policy. Achievements: HMRC tax reminders ('most people pay on time') increased compliance; 'EAST' framework (Easy, Attractive, Social, Timely) guides intervention design; NHS organ donation opt-out increased registration; pension auto-enrolment lifted savings rates. BIT model has spread globally — US, Australia, Singapore, UN."),
    ("What is the EAST framework in behavioral economics?", "BIT's EAST framework for effective nudges: Easy (simplify processes — reduce hassle, use defaults, simplify messages); Attractive (make it salient with personalisation and vivid images); Social (show that most people do the desired behaviour; harness networks); Timely (intervene at the right moment — teachable moments like marriage, new job, retirement; when people are most open to change). EAST is a practical checklist for designing nudge interventions."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of food choice?", "Food choices are strongly influenced by choice architecture. Smaller plate sizes reduce portion consumption. Positioning healthier items at eye level or first in cafeteria lines (traffic light labelling) increases selection. Calorie labelling on restaurant menus reduces calories ordered by about 38 in some studies. Smaller packaging reduces consumption. Social norms ('your neighbours ate salad') reduce unhealthy orders. Defaults (opt-in vs opt-out) in canteens dramatically shift consumption."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of energy use?", "OPOWER's home energy reports showed households their energy use versus similar neighbours, with social norm comparisons — reducing energy use by 2% per household, equivalent to a 20% price increase. Immediate feedback (real-time smart meters) reduces energy use by 5-15%. Efficiency labelling (ENERGY STAR) works as salient information. Time-of-use pricing combined with default high-efficiency settings reduces peak demand."),
    ("What is the power of social norms in behavioral change?", "Descriptive norms (what most people do) and injunctive norms (what people approve of) powerfully drive behaviour. 'Your neighbours recycled 90% of their waste' increases recycling more than factual arguments. Framing conservation as a social norm reduces hotel towel use. Voting: informing people that their neighbours voted increases turnout by 8.1 percentage points (Gerber and Green). Social norms are among the most cost-effective behaviour change tools."),
    ("What is behavioral public administration?", "Behavioral public administration applies behavioral insights to government service delivery. Applications: simplifying benefit claim forms to reduce cognitive burden on the poor; timing automatic re-enrolment to coincide with pay rises; personalising communication ('Dear John, your fine is due'); reducing form abandonment through pre-filling; and improving vaccine uptake through timely, salient appointment reminders. The approach is evidence-based and iterative."),
    ("What is the MINDSPACE framework?", "MINDSPACE (UK Cabinet Office) provides a behavioral checklist for policy: Messenger (who communicates matters — peer or authority figures); Incentives (loss aversion; immediate rewards); Norms (peer behaviour); Defaults (status quo bias); Salience (prominent and novel stimuli); Priming (prior exposure shapes responses); Affect (emotions drive decisions); Commitments (present-bias solutions); Ego (self-image and identity consistency)."),
    ("What is behavioral game theory?", "Behavioral game theory extends classical game theory to incorporate bounded rationality, social preferences, and learning. Level-k reasoning: most people are 'level-1 thinkers' (best responding to random others) rather than perfectly rational Nash equilibrium calculators. Quantal response equilibrium: players make stochastic errors inversely proportional to cost. Models of inequity aversion (Fehr and Schmidt) predict cooperation and punishment in experiments better than Nash equilibrium."),
    ("What is the equity premium puzzle?", "Equities have historically earned 6-8% more than risk-free bonds annually — far more than rational expected utility maximisation can explain, given observed risk aversion. Benartzi and Thaler's myopic loss aversion explanation: investors evaluate portfolios too frequently (narrow bracketing), making losses highly salient. Investors with a one-year horizon need a very high equity premium; those with a 30-year horizon need only 0.1%. Infrequent evaluation reduces the perceived riskiness of equities."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of online platforms?", "Digital platforms exploit multiple biases: dark patterns (using loss framing and defaults to prevent cancellation); scroll infinity (variable reward schedules — slot machine psychology); social proof (showing others have purchased); scarcity cues (5 items left!); and commitment and consistency (subscriptions). Regulatory responses include GDPR consent requirements, ban on pre-ticked opt-in boxes, and platform design transparency obligations."),
    ("What is the concept of 'irrational exuberance'?", "Robert Shiller coined 'irrational exuberance' (borrowed from Alan Greenspan) to describe speculative bubbles driven by investor overconfidence, herd behavior, and narrative — not fundamental valuation. His CAPE (Cyclically Adjusted Price-Earnings) ratio predicted the 2000 dotcom crash and the 2008 financial crisis. Shiller argues economic narratives — stories spread through social contagion — drive economic booms and busts."),
    ("What is the behavioral economics of pricing?", "Pricing strategies exploit behavioral biases: charm pricing (£9.99 instead of £10 — left-digit anchoring); partitioned pricing (base price plus fees obscures total cost); bundle pricing (mental accounting makes items feel cheaper together); price-quality signalling (high price implies quality); and drip pricing (adding charges late in the purchase process). Behaviorally informed disclosure regulations require all-in pricing."),
]

